<div><span style="white-space: pre; white-space: normal;"><font color="#0f172a">	</font></span></div><div><span style="white-space: pre; white-space: normal;">	</span></div><div>【Chief Complaint】</div><div>Progressive right upper back pain, bilateral lower limb weakness, and numbness for 2 weeks.</div><div><br></div><div>【Present Illness】</div><div>This is a 59-year-old female with past medical history of</div><div># Ovarian cancer, endometrioid carcinoma, stage IIIC, originated from endometriosis in retroperitoneum with iliac lymph node metastasis and pelvic adhesion&nbsp;</div><div>&nbsp; - status post retroperitoneal tumor biopsy (by general surgeon), right pelvic lymph node dissection and pelvic adhesiolysis on 2023/10/19&nbsp;</div><div>&nbsp; - status post chemotherapy with Paclitaxel ＋ Carboplatin ＋ Avastin (Q3W) 2023/10/25-2024/04/17, 9 cycles<span style="white-space:pre">	</span></div><div>&nbsp; - status post target therapy with Avastin 2024/05/11~2025/04/01, 15 cycles&nbsp;</div><div>&nbsp; - status post radiation therapy, 2025/04/15~2025/05/27</div><div>&nbsp; - status post immunotherapy of pembrolizumab, 2025/04/02~2025/07/19, 6 cycles&nbsp;</div><div>&nbsp; - status post chemotherapy with weekly Paclitaxel + Carboplatin + Bevacizumab since 2025/08/09, last cycle 6-3(Paclitaxel + Carboplatin ) on 2026/02/09<span style="white-space:pre">	</span></div><div>&nbsp; - with local recurrence and distant metastasis (liver, lung, bone)</div><div>&nbsp; - status post port-A indwelling on 2026/05/14</div><div>&nbsp; - status post chemotherapy with lipodox ＋ carboplatin ＋ Avastin since 2026/05/14, last cycle 2－0 on 2026/05/30</div><div># Right Hydronephrosis status post Cystoscopy + Right ureterorenoscopy (URS) + Bilateral double-J stent indwelling on 2023/10/19, Remove left and right DBJ on 2024/01/30</div><div># Retroperitoneal adenocarcinoma and right hydronephrosis status post <span style="white-space:pre">	</span></div><div>Right ureterorenoscopy (URS) + Right tumor stent indwelling on 2025/03/27, 2026/04/02</div><div># Adhesion ileus with incisional hernia status post Exploratory laparotomy, adhesiolysis + Incarcerated ventral (incisional) hernia repair with 8cm Ventralex mesh on 2026/02/12</div><div># Chronic viral hepatitis B&nbsp;</div><div># Type 2 diabetes mellitus since 2020/09</div><div><br></div><div>&nbsp; Her premorbid functional status of total independence in basic activities of daily living (BADLs) and independent community ambulation.</div><div><br></div><div>&nbsp; According to her statement, she had suffered from progressive right upper back pain, and bilateral lower limb weakness and numbness worsened progressively over two weeks. On 2026/06/13, she was scheduled for routine chemotherapy admission but was sent to the emergency department (ED) because she could no longer stand or walk due to severe pain. She denied abdominal pain, fever, dysuria, chest pain, or urinary frequency.</div><div>&nbsp; At the ED, her vital signs were stable. Physical examination revealed physical examination revealed negative straight leg raise tests bilaterally. Laboratory results showed anemia (Hb 10 g/dL), leukopenia (WBC 2,380 x103/uL), and an elevated CRP (15.39 mg/dL). TL-spine X-ray revealed a T9 compression fracture. Under the impression of T9 compression fracture, she was admitted for further evaluation and management.</div><div>&nbsp; After admission, contrast-enhanced TL spine MRI was performed and revealed multiple spinal metastases involving T8 to T11, L1, and L3, with the T9 pathological compressive fracture causing severe extradural spinal cord compression and thoracic kyphosis. Nerve conduction velocity study (NCV/F-wave) on 2026/06/15 was also compatible with sensorimotor polyneuropathy. After shared decision making with patient and family, the patient underwent T7-11 laminectomy, T9 tumor excision, T7-8 and T10-11 posterior fusion with postero-lateral bone fusion, and L2 kyphoplasty on 2026/06/17. Post-operatively, she was transferred to the surgical intensive care unit for intensive care. Her vital signs were stable and muscle power all 3, though she complained of severe back wound pain (VAS: 9.5), which was managed with adjusted analgesics (Oxycodone, Fentanyl patch). Under stable conditions, she was transferred to the ordinary ward on 2026/06/18. The Hemovac was removed on 2026/06/23, and the Foley catheter was successfully removed on 2026/06/24. Cerebrolysin was administered to enhance neurological recovery. The initial physiatric consultation on 06/23 confirmed an incomplete spinal cord injury (NLI: T8, AIS D) with neurogenic paraparesis and sensory deficits, prompting the initiation of bedside rehabilitation. Due to persistent impairments in ambulatory performance and partial dependency in ADLs, she was transferred to our PM&amp;R ward on 06/26 for intensive rehabilitation.</div><div><br></div><div>【Act】</div><div>#. T9, L2 pathological fracture, suspected ovarian cancer metastasis, s/p T9 tumor excision, T7-11 laminectomy, T7-8, T10-11 posterior fusion with Postero-lateral bone fusion, and L2 kyphoplasty (2026/06/17), with neurological deficits of bilateral lower limb weakness and numbness, NLI = T8, AIS D</div><div>#. Ovarian cancer, endometrioid carcinoma, stage IIIC, with local recurrence and distant metastasis (liver, lung, bone), s/p multiple cycles of chemotherapy, target therapy, radiotherapy, and immunotherapy</div><div><br></div><div>【InAct】</div><div>#. Normocytic Anemia, suspect cancer or chronic disease related</div><div><br></div><div>【Underly disease】</div><div># Type 2 diabetes mellitus since 2020/09</div><div># Chronic viral hepatitis B&nbsp;</div><div># Adhesion ileus with incisional hernia s/p op on 2026/02/12</div><div># Right Hydronephrosis status post op on 2025/03/27, 2026/04/02</div><div># Retroperitoneal adenocarcinoma and right hydronephrosis s/p op on 2025/03/27, 2026/04/02</div><div><br></div><div>【Plan】</div><div>#. T9, L2 pathological fracture, suspected ovarian cancer metastasis, s/p T9 tumor excision, T7-11 laminectomy, T7-8, T10-11 posterior fusion with Postero-lateral bone fusion, and L2 kyphoplasty (2026/06/17), with neurological deficits of bilateral lower limb weakness and numbness, NLI = T8, AIS D</div><div>&nbsp;- Mecobalamin 500mcg cap 1 tab TID PO&nbsp;</div><div>&nbsp;- Cerebrolysin 215.2 mg/ml 3 amp + N/S 100ml QD IVD from 06/23 to 06/30&nbsp;</div><div>&nbsp;- Chlorzoxazone 250mg/Acetaminophen 300mg 1 tab TID PO&nbsp;&nbsp;</div><div>&nbsp;- Pain control and wound care</div><div>&nbsp;- Caregiver training</div><div>&nbsp;- Check lab data on 6/29</div><div><br></div><div>#. Ovarian cancer</div><div>&nbsp;- Tenofovir alafenamide 25mg 1 tab QD PO (self&nbsp; pay)</div><div>&nbsp;- Oxycodone HCL 5mg 1 tab TIDPRN PO</div><div>&nbsp;- Fentanyl TTS 12.5mcg/hr 1 片 Q3D EXT</div><div><br></div><div># Type 2 diabetes mellitus</div><div>&nbsp;- BGMS AC/PC QD + PRN</div><div>&nbsp;- Check HbA1c on 6/29</div><div><font color="#0f172a"><br></font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">After transferral to the P&amp;R ward for intensive rehabilitation, physical therapy and occupational therapy were initiated. Laboratory data on 06/29 revealed anemia (Hb: 9.6 g/dL, Ht: 26.6%), hyponatremia (Na: 131 mEq/L), and hypokalemia (K: 3.1 mEq/L). C-spine and T-L spine X-rays showed degenerative cervical changes with C5-6 disc narrowing, a thoracic compression fracture at T9, post-operative changes at T7, T8, T10, and T11, and status post vertebroplasty at L2. The surgical wound healed well, and all staples were removed on 06/29. The central venous catheter was also removed following the completion of the Cerebrolysin therapy course on 06/29. For myofascial pain management, Clonazepam 0.5 mg 1 tablet QD PO was added. As her wound pain subsided, Oxycodone HCL 5 mg TID PRN PO was discontinued; additionally, the combination of Chlorzoxazone 250 mg/Acetaminophen 300 mg TID was switched to Acetaminophen 500 mg 1 tablet Q6H PO. For poor sleep, Zopiclone 7.5 mg 0.5 tablet HS PRN PO was prescribed. To address her mild depression and poor motivation, an oncology psychologist was consulted. Milder left neck torticollis and myofascial pain were noted, though PRN Morphine was still required occasionally due to unspecified pain and insomnia.</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a"><br></font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">&nbsp; Regarding motor function, physical therapy focused on muscle strengthening exercises, active range of motion exercises, therapeutic exercises (including mat and bed exercises), neuromuscular facilitation to address insufficient movement, and sitting/standing balance training. Furthermore, general weakness with poor endurance, as well as dizziness and vomiting following physical activity, were noted, with orthostatic hypotension highly suspected. Currently, she tolerated wheelchair sitting for up to 30 minutes. She achieved a standing posture with a walker for 20 seconds, while performing sit-to-stand transitions required minimal to moderate assistance.</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a"><br></font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">&nbsp; Cognitive attention, emotional support, and functional interventions were provided during occupational therapy, with sessions focusing on sitting endurance training, sitting/standing balance training (including sitting on the edge of the bed, sit-to-stand practice with a walker, and marching in place), upper extremity functional training, and lower extremity AROM/ankle pumping to enhance strength and endurance. Spinal orthosis (back brace) application training was also incorporated. In her activities of daily living, she demonstrated functional progression through targeted training. She improved from requiring maximal assistance to achieving modified independence in feeding. For other essential tasks, she progressed to or maintained a level of modified independence in grooming, and required minimal assistance for bed mobility, dressing, toileting, and transfers.</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a"><br></font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">&nbsp; On 07/08, new-onset left peripheral facial palsy and tongue deviation to the left were detected, accompanied by family reports of drowsiness, poor mental status, and reduced oral intake. Her consciousness level remained at baseline with a GCS of E4M6V5, and she was able to follow commands. Neurological examination showed left facial palsy and tongue deviation to the left, but extraocular movements (EOM) were intact without nystagmus or diplopia. Pupils were 3/3 mm with prompt light reflex. Muscle strength was scored as follows: RUE (P/D): 4/4+, LUE (P/D): 4+/4+, RLE (P/D): 3+/4, and LLE (P/D): 4+/4+.Regarding acute management, normal saline hydration was given, and Prednisolone was administered as suggested by the neurologist. Follow-up lab data showed persistent mild hyponatremia (131 → 130 mEq/L) and an elevation in GOT (31 → 42 U/L). Brain CT revealed no cerebral hemorrhage but showed multiple metastatic skull lesions and a left cerebellar lesion with associated vasogenic edema. A brain MRI was arranged for further evaluation. The radiologist reviewed the brain CT results and advised deferring the discussion of definitive subsequent treatment pending the brain MRI findings. Meanwhile, Megest oral suspension and Bfluid were administered to address her poor appetite and nutritional status. For pain control, Oxycodone was resumed, and oral Morphine was added. Due to advanced ovarian cancer with distant metastases and new acute intracranial lesions, she was transferred to the GYN ward for further oncology management.</font></div><div><font color="#0f172a"><br></font></div><div><font color="#0f172a"><br></font></div><div><font color="#0f172a">[Drug Profile]</font></div><div><div style=""><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/patrickwoolf/my-online-drive/main/assets/Case_20260626_0010631169_許文淑/1784344708161.jpg" class=""></div></div><div style=""><br></div><div style=""><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/patrickwoolf/my-online-drive/main/assets/Case_20260626_0010631169_許文淑/1784344743687.jpg" class=""></div><div></div>